{"name":"Mulberry","hex":"#920a4e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Mulberry","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname from Irish.","A number of places in the United States:\nAn unincorporated community in Autauga County, Alabama.\nAn unincorporated community in Chilton County, Alabama.\nAn unincorporated community in Crenshaw County, Alabama.\nA city in Crawford County, Arkansas.\nA city in Polk County, Florida. \nAn unincorporated community in Barrow County, Georgia, named for mulberry trees.\nA town in Madison Township, Clinton County, Indiana.\nA minor city in Crawford County, Kansas.\nAn unincorporated community in Shelby County, Kentucky.\nAn unincorporated community in Surry County, North Carolina.\nA census-designated place in Wilkes County, North Carolina, named for mulberry trees.\nA census-designated place in Miami Township, Clermont County, Ohio.\nA census-designated place in Adair County, Oklahoma.\nA census-designated place in Sumter County, South Carolina.\nAn unincorporated community in Lincoln County, Tennessee.\nAn unincorporated community in Fannin County, Texas.\nA number of townships in the United States, listed under Mulberry Township."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Mulberry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Morus (plant)","blocks":[{"text":"Morus is a genus of flowering plants in the family Moraceae, consisting of 17 species of deciduous trees commonly known as mulberries, growing wild and under cultivation in many warm-temperate world regions. Generally, the genus has 64 subordinate taxa; although the three of most common are referred to as white, red, and black, originating from the colour of their dormant buds, and not necessarily the fruit colour, with numerous cultivars and some taxa currently unchecked and awaiting taxonomic scrutiny. Morus alba is native to China, but is widely cultivated and often naturalised across Europe, Southern Africa, South America, and North America; it is also the species most preferred by the silkworm. It is regarded as an invasive species in Brazil, the United States and some states of Australia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Mulberries_on_tree_1.jpg/330px-Mulberries_on_tree_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morus_(plant)","wikidata":"Q44789"},{"title":"Mulberry (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The colour mulberry is a representation of the colour of mulberry jam or pie, a darker colour than that of the fresh fruit.\nTerms describing interchangeable shades, with overlapping RGB ranges, include burgundy, claret and maroon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Morus_alba_fruits.jpg/330px-Morus_alba_fruits.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mulberry_(color)","wikidata":"Q218218"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg/500px-%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg/960px-%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg/500px-%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A(MHNT)_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Black_Mulberry_Female_Flowers.jp